23
00:01:20,960 --> 00:01:22,460
First, the definitions.

24
00:01:22,460 --> 00:01:25,540
Gross yield is the annual
rent divided by the price.

25
00:01:25,540 --> 00:01:30,300
Net yield is the annual rent minus the
running costs, divided by the price.

26
00:01:30,300 --> 00:01:32,660
Gross yield is your salary before tax.

27
00:01:32,660 --> 00:01:35,160
Net yield is what lands in your account.

28
00:01:35,160 --> 00:01:37,140
Now, the costs.

29
00:01:37,140 --> 00:01:38,880
First, there's the service charge.

30
00:01:38,880 --> 00:01:41,240
On a leasehold flat,
it's often the biggest

31
00:01:41,240 --> 00:01:44,060
single cost, and it can rise year on year.

32
00:01:44,060 --> 00:01:46,960
Then there's ground rent,
which is smaller, but

33
00:01:46,960 --> 00:01:49,460
check the lease for any
increases built in.

34
00:01:49,460 --> 00:01:54,320
There's management, usually 10 to
15% of the rent if you use an agent.

35
00:01:54,880 --> 00:01:58,680
There's voids, the weeks between
tenants with no rent coming in.

36
00:01:58,680 --> 00:02:01,960
And then there's repairs and
maintenance, which never appear

37
00:02:01,960 --> 00:02:04,320
in the advert, but always
appear in your accounts.

38
00:02:04,320 --> 00:02:05,700
Here's the flat.

39
00:02:05,700 --> 00:02:10,820
£250,000, with a rent of £16,250 a year.

40
00:02:10,820 --> 00:02:13,300
That's 6.5% in the advert.

41
00:02:13,300 --> 00:02:20,840
Take off a £2,500 service
charge, £300 ground rent, £1,950

42
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:24,990
for management at 12%,
£1,354 for one month's void.

43
00:02:24,990 --> 00:02:29,110
That leaves £10,146.

44
00:02:29,110 --> 00:02:34,990
Divided by £250,000, that's 4.1%,
and that's before a single repair.

45
00:02:34,990 --> 00:02:36,840
A freehold building works differently.

46
00:02:36,840 --> 00:02:40,120
There's no service charge or ground
rent, but you're responsible

47
00:02:40,120 --> 00:02:43,040
for the whole building, including
the roof and the structure.

48
00:02:43,040 --> 00:02:46,630
Here's an illustrative
example: a freehold with a

49
00:02:46,630 --> 00:02:54,770
shop and three flats, priced at £800,000.

50
00:02:54,770 --> 00:02:59,690
With total rent of £60,000
a year, that's 7.5% gross.

51
00:02:59,690 --> 00:03:07,320
Take off £2,500 for building insurance,
£3,000 for a repairs reserve, £6,000

52
00:03:07,320 --> 00:03:13,680
for management at 10% and £3,750
for one month's void on the flats.

53
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:20,350
That leaves £44,750, which is 5.6%.

54
00:03:20,350 --> 00:03:22,380
Both lose money to costs.

55
00:03:22,380 --> 00:03:25,580
The freehold starts higher,
and with no service charge,

56
00:03:25,580 --> 00:03:28,410
less of the rent disappears
before it reaches you.

57
00:03:28,410 --> 00:03:30,150
Next, the two traps.

58
00:03:30,180 --> 00:03:33,360
First, advertised yields
are often based on the

59
00:03:33,360 --> 00:03:35,490
asking rent, not the rent being paid.

60
00:03:35,490 --> 00:03:39,910
A flat marketed at £1,400 a
month that's actually let

61
00:03:39,910 --> 00:03:43,690
at £1,250 has a lower yield
than the advert suggests.

62
00:03:43,690 --> 00:03:47,690
Always ask for the tenancy agreements
and proof the rent is being paid.

63
00:03:47,690 --> 00:03:50,710
The second is rents that include bills.

64
00:03:50,710 --> 00:03:54,990
HMO rooms are often let with bills
included, so the rent figure

65
00:03:54,990 --> 00:03:59,470
looks higher, but the landlord pays
the council tax, utilities and We

66
00:03:59,470 --> 00:04:02,170
have a building for sale in
brompton right now, as an example.

67
00:04:02,170 --> 00:04:07,330
It's 9% gross and 7.8% after the
bills the landlord pays on the The

68
00:04:07,330 --> 00:04:11,810
brochure shows both numbers, because the
second one is the one that you live on.

69
00:04:11,810 --> 00:04:14,130
Before you make an offer, ask for these.

70
00:04:14,130 --> 00:04:17,690
The service charge accounts for
the last three years, any notices

71
00:04:17,690 --> 00:04:22,170
of planned major works, the insurance
schedule, the tenancy agreements and

72
00:04:22,170 --> 00:04:25,810
the rent statements, and on a
freehold, a survey that tells

73
00:04:25,810 --> 00:04:28,590
you what the building will
need in the next five years.

74
00:04:28,590 --> 00:04:30,730
With those in front of
you, you can work out the

75
00:04:30,730 --> 00:04:33,710
net yield properly, rather than
trusting the figure on the listing.

76
00:04:33,710 --> 00:04:35,510
The last part is finance.

77
00:04:35,510 --> 00:04:37,970
Net yield tells you what
the building earns.

78
00:04:37,970 --> 00:04:42,050
Return on cash tells you what you
earn on the money you put in.

79
00:04:42,050 --> 00:04:46,310
Using the Brompton figures, with
a 30% deposit and interest-only

80
00:04:46,310 --> 00:04:52,850
finance at 6%, the income after
interest is £36,380 a year.

81
00:04:52,850 --> 00:04:56,950
That's a 10.7% return
on the cash you put in.

82
00:04:56,970 --> 00:05:00,470
Those figures are illustrative and
set out in the investment summary.

83
00:05:00,470 --> 00:05:03,270
Borrowing can increase the
return on your cash, but only

84
00:05:03,270 --> 00:05:06,250
if the net yield is higher
than your borrowing cost.

85
00:05:06,250 --> 00:05:08,470
If the net yield is below the interest

86
00:05:08,470 --> 00:05:11,190
rate, the debt costs
you money every month.

87
00:05:11,190 --> 00:05:15,240
That's why you work out the net yield
first, then decide how to finance it.

88
00:05:15,240 --> 00:05:17,640
So when you look at your next listing, ask

89
00:05:17,640 --> 00:05:20,060
for the costs before
you look at the yield.

90
00:05:20,060 --> 00:05:21,920
Gross yield gets your attention.

91
00:05:21,920 --> 00:05:24,140
Net yield is what tells
you whether to buy.

